  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-IP24-UGA177</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved protection at work and more robust and resilient social and solidarity economy</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Workers in Uganda&#x2019;s agriculture, hotels and hospitality sectors, face significant deficits in decent work and access to social protection, particularly in the informal economy. This intervention aims to increase social protection coverage and promote fundamental principles and rights at work by addressing barriers to inclusion in national systems, with a focus on improving working conditions, promoting fair wages, safe workplaces, and social dialogue.

The strategy supports the Government of Uganda&apos;s national policy on social protection and aligns with the Human Capital Development Programme of the Fourth National Development Plan (NDP IV). It promotes inclusive access to contributory social protection schemes by designing appropriate savings and social insurance products targeting informal workers. Through technical assistance, the ILO collaborates with the Ministry of Gender, Labour and Social Development, the National Social Security Fund, and social partners to strengthen institutional capacities and raise awareness on rights at work and available protections.

Capacity-building activities include training of trainers to cascade rights awareness, supporting labour market institutions to analyze and advocate for improved productivity, and enhancing women&#x2019;s leadership through gender-responsive social dialogue training. The intervention will foster more inclusive and responsive policy frameworks and contribute to DWCP Outcome 2.1 (access to social protection services) and Outcome 3.2 (advancement of fundamental principles and rights at work).
The ultimate goal is to ensure that more Ugandan workers in underserved sectors enjoy decent work and are integrated into sustainable social protection systems, contributing to a more equitable and resilient labour market.</narrative>
  </description>
